<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  HIGH-VOLUME MARKET DATA ON A SMALL DISK
  ============================================================================
  Worked configuration for this shape of problem:

      ~500 GB of market data messages per day
      100 GB of disk
      500 MB SOW snapshot
      target: keep total usage near 10 GB

  Run it with:
      AMPS_CONFIG=amps-config-market-data.xml ./server/scripts/amps.sh start

  ===========================================================================
  THE KEY POINT
  ===========================================================================
  AMPS has no log compaction for the transaction log. There is no setting that
  drops superseded same-key messages from the journal - the journal is an
  ordered, append-only record, and removing messages from the middle of it
  would break the replay and bookmark semantics it exists to provide.

  The SOW *is* the compacted view. It holds exactly one record per key, it is
  maintained continuously, and here it is 500 MB. The compaction you want has
  already happened. What is left is deciding how much history to keep beside
  it - and at 500 GB/day, on 100 GB of disk, the honest answer is "not much,
  and possibly none".

      500 GB/day = 20.8 GB/h = 356 MB/min

      journal cap    replay window (avg)    at 3x peak
        1 GB              2.9 min             ~1 min
       10 GB             28.8 min            ~10 min
       30 GB              1.4 h              ~29 min
       50 GB              2.4 h              ~48 min

  So a 10 GB cap buys roughly ten minutes of resume window during the open.
  That is fine for market data - nobody needs the ticks they missed, they need
  the current book - but it must be a decision, not a surprise.

  Full reasoning: docs/src/high-volume-market-data.md
  ===========================================================================
-->
<AMPSConfig>

    <Name>amps-market-data</Name>

    <Admin>
        <InetAddr>8085</InetAddr>
        <FileName>./stats/stats.db</FileName>
        <Interval>10s</Interval>
    </Admin>

    <Transports>
        <Transport>
            <Name>amps-tcp</Name>
            <Type>tcp</Type>
            <InetAddr>9007</InetAddr>
            <ReuseAddr>true</ReuseAddr>
            <Protocol>amps</Protocol>
        </Transport>
        <Transport>
            <Name>amps-websocket</Name>
            <Type>tcp</Type>
            <InetAddr>9008</InetAddr>
            <ReuseAddr>true</ReuseAddr>
            <Protocol>websocket</Protocol>
        </Transport>
    </Transports>

    <Logging>
        <Target>
            <Protocol>stdout</Protocol>
            <Level>info</Level>
        </Target>
    </Logging>

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      SOW
      ===================================================================
      The whole working set, one record per symbol. This is the 500 MB, and
      it is the thing that must survive a restart. Persistent durability is
      what makes current state available immediately on startup with no
      replay, however long the instance was down.

      Note the split below. "Market data" is almost always two populations
      with different durability needs, and separating them is what makes the
      disk budget work:

        market-data  huge volume, no replay value once superseded
        trades       small volume, must be replayable for audit and recovery
    -->
    <SOW>

        <!-- The high-volume stream. Keyed, persistent, queryable. -->
        <Topic>
            <Name>market-data</Name>
            <MessageType>json</MessageType>
            <Key>/symbol</Key>
            <FileName>./sow/%n.sow</FileName>
            <Durability>persistent</Durability>
            <!-- Optional: drop symbols that stop ticking, so the SOW tracks the
                 live universe rather than every symbol ever seen. Set this to
                 comfortably longer than your longest quiet period during the
                 session, or leave it out if the symbol set is fixed. -->
            <!-- <Expiration>12h</Expiration> -->
        </Topic>

        <!-- The low-volume stream that genuinely needs history. -->
        <Topic>
            <Name>trades</Name>
            <MessageType>json</MessageType>
            <Key>/tradeId</Key>
            <FileName>./sow/%n.sow</FileName>
            <Durability>persistent</Durability>
        </Topic>

    </SOW>

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      TransactionLog  -  OPTION A (active): journal only what needs replay
      ===================================================================
      `market-data` is deliberately absent. That single omission takes the
      journal from ~500 GB/day to almost nothing, and costs only the ability
      to resume that topic from a bookmark.

      What a reconnecting market data subscriber does instead:

          new Command("sow_and_subscribe").setTopic("market-data")

      which returns current state plus everything from that moment on, in one
      command, with no gap. For quotes that is not a downgrade - it is what
      you actually want, because a thirty-minute-old tick has no value.

      Disk after this change: ~500 MB of SOW, plus a small journal for trades.
    -->
    <TransactionLog>
        <JournalDirectory>./journal</JournalDirectory>
        <PreallocatedJournalFiles>2</PreallocatedJournalFiles>
        <MinJournalSize>1GB</MinJournalSize>

        <Topic>
            <Name>trades</Name>
            <MessageType>json</MessageType>
        </Topic>
    </TransactionLog>

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      OPTION B: a bounded replay window on market-data
      ===================================================================
      Use this only if a consumer genuinely needs to resume the quote stream
      from a bookmark. Add the topic to <TransactionLog> above:

          <Topic>
              <Name>market-data</Name>
              <MessageType>json</MessageType>
          </Topic>

      and enable the ageing action below by uncommenting it.

      Sizing notes, both of which matter more than they look:

        * <Age> is sized on PEAK rate, not average. At 3x the daily average,
          10 GB is about ten minutes, not twenty-nine. A window sized on the
          average is a third as long as you expect exactly at the open.

        * <Every> must be frequent enough that the cap cannot be overshot by
          more than one journal file between runs. At 356 MB/min, five minutes
          is ~1.8 GB of drift.

        * MinJournalSize sets the deletion granularity: 1 GB files against a
          10 GB cap means usage oscillates between roughly 9 and 10 GB.
          Smaller files track the cap more tightly at the cost of more
          frequent rollover.

      VERIFY THE MODULE NAMES against your AMPS version before relying on
      this. Action module and option names have moved between releases, and
      this file was written without a running server to check them. The
      surrounding <Actions><Action><On>/<Do> structure is stable. Check with:

          ./server/scripts/amps.sh validate amps-config-market-data.xml

      and look up "Actions" in the AMPS User Guide for your build if a name is
      rejected. In particular, check whether your version's journal-ageing
      action accepts a SIZE or total-bytes option as well as an age - a size
      threshold is a better fit for "keep it under 10 GB" than a time window,
      because it tracks the thing you care about and needs no re-tuning when
      volumes change.

      NEVER prune journal files with an external `rm`. AMPS tracks which files
      back which bookmark ranges; deleting them underneath it risks failed
      replays and a SOW it cannot reconcile after an unclean shutdown.

    <Actions>
        <Action>
            <On>
                <Module>amps-action-on-schedule</Module>
                <Options>
                    <Every>5m</Every>
                </Options>
            </On>
            <Do>
                <Module>amps-action-do-delete-old-journal-files</Module>
                <Options>
                    <Age>10m</Age>
                </Options>
            </Do>
        </Action>
    </Actions>
    -->

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      OPTION C: put fewer bytes in the journal
      ===================================================================
      Orthogonal to retention, and it multiplies whatever window you pick.

      If market data records are wide but each tick changes a few fields,
      publish deltas instead of whole records. The transaction log stores what
      was published, so a 10x smaller message is a 10x smaller journal - which
      turns a 10 GB / 29-minute window into a 10 GB / 4.8-hour window on the
      same disk. Measure it on your own record shape with the `journal-lab`
      demo in clients/ (see docs/src/high-volume-market-data.md for the exact
      command; it cannot be written here because XML comments may not contain
      a double hyphen).

      If your records are already minimal - a bare quote with no static
      payload - deltas will not help much and Option A is the answer.

      See docs/src/delta-updates.md and docs/src/transaction-log-sizing.md.
    -->

</AMPSConfig>
